					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/pickyerpoison" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1530329); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1530329&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610139829" alt="PickYerPoison" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1530329)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/pickyerpoison" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1530329); return false;" >PickYerPoison</a></span> <span class="odate time_1442769083 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Sep 2015 17:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2378902">
									

<p>I asked a friend of mine who is well-versed in the effects of nuclear detonations for some advice on how a nuke might affect the ISS (which I decided made for a good test subject), and here's what he said:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Probably not anything visually interesting. It's basically a lot of very hard X-rays. The best source I know of is <a href="http://www.projectrho.com/public_html/rocket/spacegunconvent.php#id--Nukes_In_Space">Nukes In Space</a>. But it's not going to be visually interesting like it would be on the ground.</p>
<p>If we rule out a hard kill, the most likely effect is the nuke pumping up the radiation background and damaging electronics and crew that way. I don't know how good the EMP shielding on the ISS is, so that's another possibility. (My guess is low, because it's international and stuff.)</p>
<p>The direct effects would be impulsive shock (if it was close enough), and lots of x-rays (if it wasn't). The first is going to shred the thing. The second isn't going to do a lot to the outside. What would be interesting is if you painted the inside.</p>
</blockquote>

							</div>

								
